      {
        "type": "thinking",
        "thinking": "I'm going for a Blizzard/Warcraft hand-painted aesthetic with gradients and mottled textures, adding glowing accents for eyes and runic details. For the design itself, I'm envisioning an original swamp troll—hunched posture, oversized arms, tusks, a mohawk, and bone fetish adornments like a necklace of skulls.\n\nNow I'm fleshing out proportions and skeleton: a roughly 2.5m standing height including the mohawk, teal-green mottled swamp skin, crimson-orange mohawk, loincloth with hide and bone fetishes. For the rig, I'm planning hips as root, spine segments, neck/head/jaw, articulated arms with digits, and digitigrade-style legs with large feet—working out whether to use a simple two-segment leg with a wedge foot or a heel-to-toe chain for the characteristic troll stance.\n\nI'm sketching out the leg chain from hip through knee to ankle, then a separate foot chain from ankle to ball to toe with three short digit segments per foot, plus four-fingered hands with two joints each, keeping the total joint budget around 160. For the rest pose I'm going with a neutral standing A-pose, hunched torso, arms slightly out and down, hands open with spread fingers, and I'm roughing out the vertical coordinates—feet at ground level, ankles around 0.16m, knees near 0.78m, hips around 1.2m—to get the hunched troll proportions right.\n\nNow I'm reconsidering the leg structure to give it a more digitigrade, Warcraft-troll stance: a three-segment limb from hip through knee to hock, ending in a foot segment with a sole offset, adjusting bend directions and lengths so the knee juts forward and the heel sits back convincingly.\n\nWith 4 lengths and 5 points, there are 3 inner joints, but the sole-setting rule removes one direction, leaving fewer bend values needed—matching the bird example's 3-item list.\n\nRather than fighting the limb helper's segment math, I'll simplify by directly hand-placing the leg points as a polyline: hip, knee, hock, ball, toe. That avoids the complexity of configuring limb lengths and angles correctly, even though hand-tuning isn't ideal, it's a pragmatic shortcut here.\n\nChecking the reach distance confirms the leg will bend naturally within range, giving a digitigrade metatarsus angled steeply down with toes extending flat forward from the ball, plus a heel pad for realism. I'll plan to preview this and iterate, then position the hunched torso with hips around y≈1.25, z=-0.1.\n\nNow I'm mapping the spine upward and forward through spine1, chest, and neck base, placing the head forward and low between the shoulders with a troll-like elongated jaw and tusks, keeping total height with mohawk around 2.55 to feel imposing but not oversized for a swamp troll. I'm sketching long arms from shoulder to big-handed wrist, reaching down near the knees, with a slight outward rest pose so limbs don't clip the torso.\n\nNow I'm designing the body structure as a loft along a spine chain, laying out hip, spine, and neck joints with catmull-spline control points that curve forward and upward, naming each segment (spine1-3, neck1-2) so the chain spans correctly into a forward-aimed head joint.\n\nI'm laying out loft stations for pelvis, waist, chest, and shoulders with their widths and heights, planning to interpolate between them using a catmull curve through the station centers.\n\nI want to add volume with extra pot-belly and deltoid/trapezius spheres layered onto the torso loft rather than modifying the loft itself. Given the full skeleton complexity, I should organize this into clear helper functions and sections, iterating with preview renders, and I'm settling on a saturated, hand-painted Warcraft-style palette.\n\nFor skin, I'm going with a murky swamp teal-green base with darker shadow tones and a lighter yellow-green belly for countershading, plus darker warts and moss-colored lichen patches. For the mohawk, I'm leaning toward a crimson-to-yellow gradient on spiky card shapes to give it that painted, fiery look.\n\nFor the mohawk I'll build 14 swept spike shapes running along the sagittal crest from forehead to nape, each gradient-painted from deep red to orange tips with binding rings, plus textured hair-strand cards layered in for extra detail. Tusks get an ivory base with darker grain and a yellowed stain near the root, done with gradient paint.\n\nNow I'm picking the material palette: bone tones, leather in dark browns, a red-brown cloth loincloth with tribal pattern SVGs, painted white and yellow war-paint stripes as face/chest decals, rusty brass and copper metal accents with verdigris patina, and turquoise/red beads. For the eyes I'm going with a glowing yellow-orange with a dark pupil using a glow effect.\n\nI'm then sketching out the construction plan: the body as torso, belly, shoulders, and hunched trap muscles; the head with a rounded skull, heavy brow ridge, jutting underbite jaw with curved tusks and small upper teeth, long swept-back pointed ears with piercings, warty bumps, and a mohawk; then a neck layer with draped bone/claw/skull necklaces and a bone fetish shoulder pauldron.\n\nContinuing outward I'm planning the opposite shoulder with bone spikes on a leather strap, boar-skull decoration, arms with clawed four-fingered hands, wrist wraps and bone bracelets, legs with knee wraps and ankle bone rings and clawed toes, and a belt with a loincloth bearing painted flaps and dangling fetishes -- shrunken heads, voodoo dolls, totems, pouches, vials, trophy skulls, and a leaf/moss skirt. I'm also adding swamp texture details like moss and lichen patches, reeds, and a mud-paint gradient darkening toward the lower legs, maybe with a small frog perched on the shoulder for character.\n\nI'm deciding to keep the figure's hands empty in rest pose but hang a bone club and fetish totem from the belt instead of holding a weapon, and I'm planning decal-based tattoos -- tribal chest patterns, forearm markings, and painted facial stripes -- applied as transparent SVG textures onto the torso mesh. For the overall rendering style I'm aiming for a hand-painted look with baked shading: countershaded gradients, mottled skin tones, and subtle painted ambient occlusion in creases like the armpits.\n\nWriting paint shader code...\n\nI'm thinking through the triangle budget, aiming for around 60k total across roughly 1000 parts, and starting to sketch the head geometry -- a forward-aimed skull with proportions suited to a troll-like creature, around 0.34 wide, 0.4 long, and 0.38 tall.\n\nNow I'm detailing the cranium shape and brow ridge, then building out the face: a long, pointed troll-style nose swept forward and down with nostrils, an upper snout, and a separate lower jaw piece with a prominent chin and curved tusks sprouting from the jaw sides.\n\nAdding teeth, cheekbones, glowing amber eyes with slit pupils, long pointed backward-angled ears with ring earrings, thick swept lips, and dark red mouth interior with a tongue. I'm starting on the mohawk crest now.\n\nWriting hair gradient paint...\n\nAlso planning the mohawk base as a flat strip along the scalp in dark red with shaved sides showing skin, plus individual hair strand cards layered on top.\n\nFor bone fetishes, I'm working out a necklace draped around the neck and chest using looped bone beads as merged spheres, with pendants like tusks, claws, and a small skull hanging off a leather cord path.\n\nFor the shoulder armor, I'm planning a large beast skull pauldron (pig/boar-like, with horns) built from spheres, snout, and horn cones on the left shoulder, crossed with leather straps, and a feather-and-bone bundle on the right shoulder, plus a wide leather belt with a skull-face bone buckle and hanging bone pieces.\n\nI'm also considering a back totem: a tall wooden pole rising from the lower spine to above the head, stacked with skulls, feathers, and glowing charms, strapped on with a leather harness for a strong Warcraft-style silhouette, while being mindful it stays within the bounding height limits.\n\nFor hands, I'm planning each finger as a small chain of joints off the palm, parented to a wrist joint, with a helper function to build them per-direction, and resting fingers slightly curled and spread apart in a natural hanging pose.\n\nArms hang downward with fingers splaying laterally and curling slightly forward; I'll just use plain world coordinates with sx=1/-1 for left/right to avoid confusing myself with the earlier sign convention.\n\nEach finger curls as a 4-point chain from base downward with forward curl, built as a tapered sweep with rounded caps, small knuckle bumps, and a claw spike at the tip -- naming joints like indexL1 through indexL4 for each digit.\n\nBeyond the 10 finger sweeps plus 6 toe sweeps, I'm thinking about the palm and wrist mass: a scaled sphere for the hand, a tapered forearm tube, and an arm chain running shoulder-elbow-wrist with specific coordinates scaled by body side, ending in a hand tip joint.\n\nFingers parent to wristL, and I'm shaping the arm's radius profile with a thicker bicep and forearm tapering to a slimmer wrist, adding a deltoid sphere at the shoulder. I'm placing the shoulder joint inside the torso where the arm tube begins, and setting the torso loft shoulders at x=0.55 with a width of 1.05.\n\nNow I'm working out the leg chain structure: hip at x=±0.3, with joints named hipL, kneeL, hockL, and a tip joint ballL, giving three spans from hip to knee to hock to ball, with toes parenting off the ball joint.\n\nFor the foot since it's digitigrade, I'm placing the ball joint at the floor contact point, designing three forward toes plus a dewclaw, sizing toe radius around 0.06-0.07 so the sole sits flush at y=0.\n\nNo tail since trolls don't have one, just a loincloth and a pot belly sphere for that chunky look. Now I need to check the loft and chain function signatures in the builder source to set up spine stations properly.\n\n",
